# Git LFS for Unreal binary assets.
#
# .uasset and .umap are binary and get large fast. Without LFS, every edit of a
# map stores a whole new copy in history and the repo becomes unusable within a
# few weeks. Run `git lfs install` once before the first commit.

*.uasset  filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.umap    filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.ubulk   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.uexp    filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.upack   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

# Source art
*.fbx     filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.tga     filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.wav     filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

# Heightmaps are deliberately NOT LFS.
#
# They were committed as ordinary blobs, and declaring them LFS while the
# history holds plain content makes every clone with git-lfs installed report
# them as locally modified: the clean filter turns the working file into a
# pointer, which does not match the stored blob. `git pull` then refuses to
# merge over "local changes" nobody made.
#
# Keeping them as blobs is also the right call on the merits. They are
# generated — tools/export-heightmap.mjs in the callofbooty repo rebuilds them
# from the traced geography — so they are reproducible rather than precious, and
# there are three of them, not three thousand.
# The same goes for the city plan's packed building buffer, which is generated
# by tools/export-city.mjs and is the same kind of thing: reproducible, small,
# and much better as a plain blob than as an LFS pointer nobody can pull.
*.png     -text
*.r16     -text
*.raw     -text
*.bin     -text

# Text that should stay text and diff normally
*.cpp     text
*.h       text
*.cs      text
*.ini     text
*.uproject text
*.glb  filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
